    "status": "SUCCESS",
    "response": "In short: **no, you should not read anything into the fact that the panel was made up entirely of four women.** It is almost certainly not a sign of bias or an agenda, especially since both you and the successful candidate are women.\n\nHere is a breakdown of why that is, along with what is likely going on with the decision and the communication delay:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Why the panel was all women\n* **Demographics and service burdens:** Committee assignments in universities are typically determined by departmental rotation, availability, seniority, or institutional rules requiring female representation on interview panels. Depending on the size of the department, this frequently results in panels that happen to be all-female or all-male without any deliberate design.\n* **No inherent conflict:** An all-female panel does not suggest a bias against women candidates, nor does it inherently favor one profile of candidate over another. \n\n---\n\n### 2. Why the other candidate may have been selected\nOn paper, your traditional academic metrics (completed PhD, 6 papers, 4 years of university teaching) appear stronger than those of someone finishing their PhD with 2 papers. However, academic hiring decisions—especially for lecturer roles—often turn on factors beyond raw publication count:\n\n* **Departmental strategic needs:** The department may have had a specific gap they needed to fill, such as teaching applied/business economics, supervising industry-linked student capstones, or developing external partnerships. In those cases, \"strong experience from industry\" can outweigh academic tenure.\n* **Sub-field alignment:** Search committees often prioritize exact curriculum fit (e.g., econometrics vs. behavioral economics vs. financial regulation) over general credentials.\n* **Perceived trajectory and cost:** Entry-level lecturer appointments are sometimes budgeted at the lower end of the pay scale. Committees occasionally lean toward fresh PhD graduates who fit entry salary bands and whose career trajectory they feel they can shape, versus more experienced candidates who might be viewed as overqualified or seeking a higher pay step.\n* **Interview performance & \"job talk\":** With a final shortlist of three, candidates are usually considered roughly equal on minimum qualifications. The final decision often comes down to the teaching demonstration, interpersonal rapport, and how the panel envisioned each candidate fitting into department life.\n\n---\n\n### 3. The factor of age\nYou noted that you are in your forties and the other candidate is finishing her PhD. While age discrimination does unfortunately exist in hiring (and sometimes manifests in academia as an unstated preference for \"early-career\" candidates), the gender composition of the panel is unrelated to this. If age played any subtle role, it would typically stem from assumptions about salary, career stage, or departmental hierarchy, not the gender of the interviewers.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Why you haven't received written feedback yet\nA two-week silence after requesting feedback from a public university is completely standard:\n\n* **The contract is still pending:** Public universities usually cannot release formal feedback or close out the search until the preferred candidate has signed the formal contract and cleared background checks.\n* **HR constraints:** Many public institutions strictly regulate what interviewers can put in writing to minimize legal liability. Feedback often has to be vetted by HR or the Dean's office before being sent.\n* **Administrative pace:** Academic bureaucracy moves slowly, especially during peak term periods.\n\n---\n\n### Recommended next steps\n\n* **Do not raise panel composition:** Bringing up the panel's gender makeup will likely be perceived as defensive or searching for grievance where standard procedural processes were followed.\n* **Follow up politely:** If you don't hear back after three to four weeks from your initial request, send a brief, courteous follow-up to the search chair:\n  > *\"I understand you are busy wrapping up the hiring round. When time permits, I would still greatly value any constructive feedback on my application and interview to help inform my future applications.\"*\n* **Request a brief phone or video call:** Committees are often much more candid and helpful over a 10-minute informal phone call than in written correspondence, which they know could be scrutinized legally.\n",
